---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import core from '../data/core.json';
import sources from '../data/sources.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const c = core.counts;
const relCounts = {};
for (const s of sources.sources) relCounts[s.reliability] = (relCounts[s.reliability] || 0) + 1;
const typeCounts = {};
for (const s of sources.sources) typeCounts[s.type] = (typeCounts[s.type] || 0) + 1;
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Buddhist Bridges: India–Korea Buddhist Mediators Dataset (V3.0)',
  description: 'Curated, source-graded dataset of documented mediators of India–Korea Buddhist exchange, 300–2026 CE: 76 people (43 carried), 41 travels, 30 texts, 31 institutions, 19 events, 187 graded sources, with per-record confidence, status, and date-precision fields.',
  creator: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Mohd Bilal Khan' },
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
  version: '3.0',
  datePublished: '2026-08-17',
});
---
<Base title="Data & Method" current="data" description="Methodology, source registry, uncertainty conventions, limitations, downloads and citation for the Buddhist Bridges dataset (V3.0, frozen).">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={jsonld}></script>
  <style>
    .dl { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 0.8rem; margin: 1.2rem 0; font-family: var(--sans); }
    .cite {
      background: var(--codebg); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
      font: 0.85rem/1.6 var(--mono); position: relative; overflow-x: auto;
    }
    .glossary dt { font-weight: 600; margin-top: 0.9rem; }
    .glossary dd { margin: 0.1rem 0 0; color: var(--muted); }
  </style>
  <div class="wrap" style="padding-top: 3rem;">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
    <h1>Data &amp; method</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Everything on this site is built from one frozen, checksummed dataset. This page explains
      how it was made, what it can and cannot support, and how to cite or rebuild it.
    </p>

    <h2>The dataset</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Buddhist Bridges V3.0</strong> (frozen 2026-08-17) records documented mediators of
      Buddhist exchange between India and Korea, 4th century CE–2026: {c.people_total} people
      ({c.people_carried} carried in scope after classification), {c.travels} travels, {c.texts}
      texts, {c.institutions} institutions, {c.events} events, and {c.sources} sources — every
      source graded (AUTHORITATIVE / ACADEMIC / INSTITUTIONAL / POPULAR) and dated.
    </p>
    <p>
      The governing rule of collection: <em>no relationship is recorded as influential without
      documentary evidence — association with both countries is not influence.</em> Candidates
      that failed the evidence bar are preserved as documented negatives, not deleted.
    </p>

    <h2>How records are qualified</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Confidence — HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW / SPECULATIVE</dt>
      <dd>Grades the source basis of a record, per person, travel, relationship, and text.
      Confidence tracks how close the sources are to the event, not historical importance:
      the ancient cohort is mostly MEDIUM (hagiographic sources), the modern cohort mostly HIGH
      (contemporary documentation).</dd>
      <dt>Status — VERIFIED / PARTIAL / UNRESOLVED / LEGENDARY</dt>
      <dd>Flags record completeness. Legendary material (Heo Hwang-ok, King Suro) is retained,
      labelled, and excluded from analysis by default.</dd>
      <dt>Date precision — YEAR / MONTH / DAY / CENTURY / APPROX / UNKNOWN</dt>
      <dd>Prevents invented precision. Records with no safe date are listed off-axis on the
      timeline, never assigned a position.</dd>
      <dt>Scope — KEEP / ADAPT / CONTEXT / EXCLUDE</dt>
      <dd>The Buddhist-relevance classification of every V2 record; "carried" = KEEP + ADAPT.
      The excluded rows remain in the dataset and are inspectable.</dd>
      <dt>Single-source flag</dt>
      <dd>Eight carried people and eight events rest on one source each; they are hatched or
      badged in every visualization on this site.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Known limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Joseon void (B3) is a finding under criteria, not a proof of zero contact.</strong>
      Eleven candidate rows were examined and excluded; the one live lead (Yi Kyu-gyŏng's India
      entries) is an open research question.</li>
      <li><strong>Source-density asymmetry.</strong> The modern era is news-documented
      ({typeCounts['NEWS'] ?? 0} of {c.sources} sources are press); the ancient core rests on a
      small primary/archival base. Cross-era intensity comparisons are qualified everywhere by
      the record-density ribbon.</li>
      <li><strong>Korean-language source dominance.</strong> Korean Buddhist press is
      over-represented; Indian-side corroboration is a stated priority for the next collection
      round.</li>
      <li><strong>No women appear before 1990.</strong> The register documents this absence; it
      cannot distinguish absence-from-record from absence-in-history.</li>
      <li><strong>The carried set under-represents the translation layer.</strong> The colonial
      translators of Tagore are excluded-scope figures (their connection is textual, not
      Buddhist-India); the textual layer must be read through the texts table.</li>
      <li><strong>Descriptive analysis only.</strong> With 43 carried people, no inferential
      statistics or community detection are used anywhere.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reproducibility</h2>
    <p>
      The dataset lives as plain-text Python modules compiled deterministically into CSVs
      (<code>build_dataset.py</code>, 0 errors / 0 warnings), audited
      (<code>audit_v3.py</code>), and frozen with SHA-256 checksums. This site consumes only a
      JSON export tier (<code>analysis/export_web.py</code>) that verifies the frozen manifest
      before every build — the site cannot silently change the data, and the data cannot
      silently change the site.
    </p>

    <h2>Downloads</h2>
    <div class="dl">
      <a class="door" href={`${base}/data/people.json`} download><p class="door-title">people.json</p><p>The 43 carried mediators with register fields and resolved sources</p></a>
      <a class="door" href={`${base}/data/story.json`} download><p class="door-title">story.json</p><p>Cohorts, directions, mechanisms, journeys, events</p></a>
      <a class="door" href={`${base}/data/timeline.json`} download><p class="door-title">timeline.json</p><p>All 182 dated marks with uncertainty fields</p></a>
      <a class="door" href={`${base}/data/sources.json`} download><p class="door-title">sources.json</p><p>The complete graded source registry ({c.sources})</p></a>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      The full frozen CSV release (13 files + SHA-256 manifest) and build scripts will be
      deposited with a DOI on Zenodo; until the deposit is live, the JSON exports above are the
      citable web release. Data: CC-BY-4.0 · Code: MIT.
    </p>

    <h2>Source registry at a glance</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Reliability</th><th class="num">Sources</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {Object.entries(relCounts).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]).map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr><td>{k}</td><td class="num">{v}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h2 id="cite">Cite this project</h2>
    <div class="cite" id="citation">Khan, Mohd Bilal (2026). <em>Buddhist Bridges: Mapping India–Korea Networks of People, Places,
Texts, and Ideas</em> (dataset V3.0, frozen 2026-08-17). Digital humanities project.
https://[site-url] — data CC-BY-4.0, code MIT. DOI: pending Zenodo deposit.</div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top: 0.6rem;">
      A machine-readable <code>CITATION.cff</code> ships with the repository; the DOI line will
      be updated at deposit time.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
